%[text] # Part 2 - Bringing Existing Verilog into Simulink
%[text] You already have proven RTL. Moving to Simulink does not mean rewriting it. There are three different things you can do with an existing `.v` file, and they answer three different questions:
%[text:table]
%[text] |  | **A. importhdl** | **B. BlackBox subsystem (+ DocBlock)** | **C. HDL Cosimulation (Part 4)** |
%[text] | --- | --- | --- | --- |
%[text] | Question it answers | "Turn my RTL into a Simulink model" | "Reuse my RTL unchanged inside a new Simulink design" | "Run my real RTL against the Simulink model" |
%[text] | What happens to the Verilog | **Translated** into native Simulink blocks | **Copied verbatim** into the generated HDL and instantiated | **Executed** in an HDL simulator (Vivado Simulator, Questa, Xcelium, VCS) |
%[text] | What Simulink simulates | The translated blocks (fast, no simulator) | A behavioral model you put inside the subsystem - or a cosim block (C) | The actual RTL, cycle-accurate, through the simulator |
%[text] | Generated HDL | New HDL written by HDL Coder from the blocks | Your original module + HDL Coder code around it | None - verification only |
%[text] | Edit the design in Simulink? | Yes - it is now a normal model | No - edit the Verilog text | No - edit the Verilog text |
%[text] | Language support | Synthesizable Verilog / SystemVerilog / VHDL subset, **one clock**, no multirate | Anything your synthesis tool accepts (incl. vendor primitives, encrypted IP) | Anything your simulator accepts (incl. testbench constructs, XCI IP) |
%[text] | Products | HDL Coder | HDL Coder | HDL Verifier + HDL simulator |
%[text] | Typical use | Migrating small/medium legacy blocks into MBD so they can be optimized, retargeted, re-verified | Keeping a golden or 3rd-party IP block as-is while new logic is designed in Simulink | Proving the RTL matches the model; reusing Simulink test benches for RTL |
%[text:table]
%[text] **Short answer to "importhdl vs. DocBlock/BlackBox with cosimulation":** importhdl **converts** your Verilog - after that the Verilog file is no longer used. BlackBox + DocBlock **reuses** your Verilog - Simulink never looks at it, it only instantiates it in the generated code. Cosimulation **verifies** your Verilog - it runs it. In practice B and C are used together: the BlackBox carries the RTL into code generation and an HDL Cosimulation block inside it simulates the real RTL instead of a hand-made behavioral model.
%[text] The legacy RTL used in this part (`hdl_src/fir4_legacy.v`) is the same filter as Part 1, hand-written with shift-and-add and a registered output (1 clock latency):
%[text] ```verilog
%[text] module fir4_legacy (
%[text]     input  wire               clk,
%[text]     input  wire               rst,      // synchronous, active-high
%[text]     input  wire               clk_en,
%[text]     input  wire signed [15:0] x_in,     // Q1.15
%[text]     output reg  signed [18:0] y_out     // Q4.18
%[text] );
%[text]     reg signed [15:0] x_d1, x_d2, x_d3;
%[text]     wire signed [18:0] acc = x_in + ((x_d1 <<< 1) + x_d1)
%[text]                                   + ((x_d2 <<< 1) + x_d2) + x_d3;
%[text]     always @(posedge clk)
%[text]         if (rst)         begin x_d1 <= 0; x_d2 <= 0; x_d3 <= 0; y_out <= 0; end
%[text]         else if (clk_en) begin x_d1 <= x_in; x_d2 <= x_d1; x_d3 <= x_d2; y_out <= acc; end
%[text] endmodule
%[text] ```
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Contents"}
%%
%[text] ## A. importhdl - convert Verilog into a Simulink model
%[text] Reference: [Import HDL Code and Generate Simulink Model](https://www.mathworks.com/help/hdlcoder/ug/hdl-import-import-hdl-code-and-generate-simulink-model.html)
%[text] ### A1. Run the import
%[text] HDL import has no app; it is a single command. It parses the file, checks syntax and semantics (port connections, sensitivity lists ...), identifies clock, reset and clock enable, and writes `hdlimport/<top>/<top>.slx` in the current folder.
demoRoot = fileparts(matlab.desktop.editor.getActiveFilename);
cd(demoRoot)
importhdl(fullfile("hdl_src", "fir4_legacy.v"))
%%
%[text] ### A2. Look at what was generated
%[text] The log shows `ClkName::clk`, `RstName::rst`, `ClkEnbName::clk_en`: these ports disappear and become Simulink's implicit clock, reset and enable. Only the data ports `x_in` and `y_out` remain.
%[text] ![Imported model - top level](text:image:10ed01)
%[text] Inside, every `reg` became a Delay block named after the register (`x_d1_reg`, `y_out_1_reg`), and every `<<<` and `+` became a shift or add block. It is a faithful, netlist-like translation of the RTL - correct, but it reads like the RTL, not like the algorithm. This is normal: importhdl preserves structure, it does not recover intent.
%[text] ![Imported model - inside](text:image:7ec802)
%[text] Note the port types: `int16` in, `sfix19` out. **Verilog has no binary point**, so the import cannot know that `x_in` was meant as Q1\.15. You add that meaning back in Simulink.
%%
%[text] ### A3. Prove the import matches the Part 1 design
%[text] Open `models/fir_import_compare.slx`. It drives the imported model and the Part 1 design with the same stimulus:
%[text] - **Model** block (Ports & Subsystems) with Model name `fir4_legacy` references the imported file, so a re-import updates the test bench automatically.
%[text] - Two **Data Type Conversion** blocks set to *Input and output to have equal* = `Stored Integer (SI)` reinterpret the bits: Q1\.15 -> `int16` going in, `sfix19` -> Q4\.18 coming out.
%[text] - A **Unit Delay** after the Part 1 filter accounts for the registered output of the Verilog (1 clock latency) \
%[text] ![Comparison test bench](text:image:0fe403)
addpath(fullfile(demoRoot, "hdlimport", "fir4_legacy"), fullfile(demoRoot, "models"));
open_system("fir_import_compare")
out = sim("fir_import_compare");
maxError = max(abs(double(out.logsout.get("err").Values.Data)))
%[text] ![Imported RTL vs Part 1 model](text:image:55ce04)
%[text] Do not import HDL that HDL Coder itself generated - keep the original model instead.
%%
%[text] ### A4. Does my Verilog need a special structure?
%[text] Mostly no. If it is clean, synthesizable, single-clock RTL, `importhdl` takes it as written. What it rejects are the constructs that do not map to one synchronous Simulink rate. We ran 24 typical RTL patterns through `importhdl` in R2026a; the files and a results table are in `importhdl_examples`, so you can try them yourself. Reference: [Supported Verilog Constructs for HDL Import](https://www.mathworks.com/help/hdlcoder/ug/supported-verilog-constructs-for-hdl-import.html) and [Verilog Dataflow Modeling with HDL Import](https://www.mathworks.com/help/hdlcoder/ug/verilog-dataflow-modeling-with-hdl-import.html).
%[text] **Imports as written:**
%[text:table]
%[text] | Pattern | Becomes in Simulink |
%[text] | --- | --- |
%[text] | `always @(posedge clk)` with synchronous `if (rst)` | Delay blocks with Simulink's global reset |
%[text] | Asynchronous reset: `posedge rst` or `negedge rst_n` | Delay blocks (reset handled implicitly) |
%[text] | `case`, `casez`, `if/else`, `?:` | Switch / Multiport Switch blocks |
%[text] | FSM with `localparam` state codes | Delay (state register) + switches + comparators |
%[text] | `for` loops in `always`, and `generate for` that assigns every index | Unrolled blocks |
%[text] | `function` | Inlined blocks |
%[text] | Module hierarchy with parameter overrides `#(.W(16))` | Subsystem hierarchy |
%[text] | `*`, `/`, `$signed`, shifts, reduction, concatenation | Product, shift and bit-operation blocks |
%[text] | Synchronous RAM (`reg [15:0] mem [0:15]` written on `posedge clk`) | RAM block |
%[text] | ROM via `case` or `initial` with constant assignments | Constant + Multiport Switch |
%[text] | Compiler directives (include / define) | Resolved during parsing |
%[text] | `$display` and `#delays` | Ignored |
%[text:table]
%[text] **Rejected - change the RTL, or keep it as a BlackBox (method B):**
%[text:table]
%[text] | Pattern | importhdl message | What to do |
%[text] | --- | --- | --- |
%[text] | Two clocks (`clk_a`, `clk_b`) in one module | *Multiple clocks are not supported* | Split per clock domain. Import each domain separately, or black-box the CDC logic |
%[text] | `posedge clk` and `negedge clk` in one module | *sensitivity to different clock edges ... not supported* | Use one edge, or move the negedge logic into its own module |
%[text] | Sync and async reset styles in one module | *Synchronous and Asynchronous circuit realization is not supported within same module* | Use one reset style per module |
%[text] | Incomplete `if` / `case` in combinational logic | *Latch logic is detected* | Add the `else` / `default` (good RTL practice anyway) |
%[text] | `task` | *'task' is not supported* | Rewrite as a `function` |
%[text] | `inout` / tri-state | *Inout port type is not supported* | Keep the I/O buffer in the top-level RTL; import only the core |
%[text] | Vector partly assigned inside `generate` and partly outside it | *Only certain indices ... have been assigned* | Assign all indices in the same construct |
%[text] | SystemVerilog file (`.sv`, `always_ff`, `logic`) | Not accepted - R2026a `importhdl` takes `'Verilog'` or `'VHDL'` | Convert to Verilog-2001, or black-box it |
%[text:table]
%[text] **Two options worth knowing:**
%[text] - `clockBundle` - tell the importer what your clock/reset/enable are called if the names are unusual. For example, a synchronous clear called `sclr` otherwise imports as an ordinary data input.
%[text] - `blackBoxModule` - import a design but keep selected sub-modules (vendor primitives, a module with a `task`, a CDC block) as black boxes. The rest becomes Simulink and the black boxes are instantiated as-is - methods A and B combined \
%[text] Other limits: no import from read-only folders, and not on macOS.
%%
%[text] ## B. BlackBox subsystem + DocBlock - reuse Verilog verbatim
%[text] Here the Verilog is not converted. HDL Coder treats a subsystem as an empty box with the right ports and instantiates your module in the generated code. Optionally a **DocBlock** stores the Verilog text inside the `.slx`, so the model is self-contained.
%[text] The finished example is `models/fir_blackbox.slx`. The DUT `HDL_DUT` contains the legacy filter (black box) followed by a **new** block designed in Simulink (round + saturate back to Q1\.15) - a typical "old IP + new feature" situation.
%[text] ![DUT: legacy black box + new Simulink logic](text:image:676205)
%[text] ### B1. Create the black-box subsystem
%[text] 1. Add a **Subsystem** and give it any name **other than** the module name (here `legacy_fir_bb` - HDL Coder warns about a name collision otherwise). Name its ports exactly like the Verilog data ports: `x_in`, `y_out`. Do not add clock/reset/enable ports.
%[text] 2. Right-click it > **HDL Code** > **HDL Block Properties**. Set **Architecture** = `BlackBox`.
%[text] 3. Set `EntityName` = `fir4_legacy`, `ClockInputPort` = `clk`, `ResetInputPort` = `rst`, `ClockEnableInputPort` = `clk_en` so the instance matches your module's port names \
%[text] ![HDL Block Properties: BlackBox](text:image:863906)
%[text] ### B2. Give Simulink something to simulate
%[text] HDL Coder ignores the contents of a BlackBox, but Simulink still simulates them. Put a behavioral model inside - here the Part 1 filter plus one Unit Delay for the output register. (In Part 4 you can replace it with an HDL Cosimulation block that runs the real `fir4_legacy.v`.)
%[text] ### B3. Carry the Verilog in a DocBlock (optional)
%[text] 1. Inside the black box add a **DocBlock** (Model-Wide Utilities). Double-click it and paste the complete Verilog module.
%[text] 2. Right-click the DocBlock > **HDL Code** > **HDL Block Properties**: Architecture = `HDLText`, TargetLanguage = `Verilog` \
%[text] ![Inside the black box](text:image:56e407)
%[text] ![HDL Block Properties: DocBlock as HDLText](text:image:982c08)
%[text] Without a DocBlock the flow is identical; you just add `fir4_legacy.v` to your synthesis project yourself (for IP core generation: **Additional source files** in task 3\.2 of the HDL Workflow Advisor).
%[text] ### B4. Generate HDL
%[text] Generate HDL for `fir_blackbox/HDL_DUT` (HDL Coder app > **Generate HDL**). You get two files: `HDL_DUT.v` written by HDL Coder, and `fir4_legacy.v` copied from the DocBlock. The generated top simply instantiates your module:
%[text] ```verilog
%[text] fir4_legacy u_legacy_fir_bb (.clk(clk),
%[text]                              .clk_en(enb),
%[text]                              .rst(reset),
%[text]                              .x_in(x_in),  // sfix16_En15
%[text]                              .y_out(legacy_fir_bb_out1)  // sfix19_En18
%[text]                              );
%[text] ```
open_system(fullfile(demoRoot, "models", "fir_blackbox.slx"))
makehdl("fir_blackbox/HDL_DUT")
%[text] **The catch:** the behavioral model and the Verilog are two separate descriptions. If they disagree, Simulink shows one thing and the FPGA does another. That is exactly the gap that HDL cosimulation closes - see Part 4.
%%
%[text] ## Which one should you use?
%[text] - Legacy block you will keep evolving, simple single-clock RTL: **importhdl**, then refactor the imported model toward the algorithm and verify with a comparison test bench like A3.
%[text] - Proven block or vendor IP you will not touch: **BlackBox** (+ DocBlock if you want the code inside the model).
%[text] - Any time Simulink and RTL must be shown to agree: **HDL Cosimulation** (Part 4), possibly placed inside the BlackBox \

